/Author (John Heil)
/CreationDate (D:20190811095834+05'30')
/Keywords <>
/Subject
(The book is intended as a reader-friendly introduction to issues in the philosophy of mind, including mental\205physical causal interaction, computational models of thought, the relation minds bear to brains, and assorted -isms: behaviorism, dualism, eliminativism, emergentism, functionalism, materialism, neutral monism, and panpsychism. The Fourth Edition reintroduces a chapter on Donald Davidson and a discussion of \217Non-Cartesian Dualism\220, along with a wholly new chapter on emergence and panpsychism. A concluding chapter draws together material in earlier chapters and offers what the author regards as a plausible account of the mind\220s place in nature. Suggested readings at the conclusion of each chapter have been updated, with a focus on accessible, non-technical material. Key Features of the Fourth EditionIncludes a new chapter, \217Emergence and Panpsychism\220 \(Chapter 13\), reflecting growing interest in these areas Reintroduces and updates a chapter on Donald Davidson, \217Radical Interpretation\220 \(Chapter 8\), which was excised from the previous edition Updates \217Descartes\220s Legacy\220 \(Chapter 3\) to include a discussion of E. J. Lowe\220s arresting \217Non-Cartesian Dualism\220, also removed from the previous edition Includes a highly revised final chapter, which draws together much of the previous material and sketches a plausible account of the mind\220s place in nature Updated \217Suggested Reading\220 lists at the end of each chapter )
/Title (Philosophy of Mind; A Contemporary Introduction; Fourth Edition)
/ModDate (D:20220218232846Z)
/Producer
(3-Heights\222 PDF Optimization Shell 6.3.1.5 \(http://www.pdf-tools.com\))
>>
